---
import ToolPage from '../../layouts/ToolPage.astro';
import Readiness from '../../islands/Readiness';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'Who has to meet these requirements?', a: 'Everyone sending to Gmail, Yahoo or Outlook, with a stricter set for anyone sending more than about 5,000 messages a day to a single provider. The bulk threshold is per provider, and it is easier to cross than most senders expect.' },
  { q: 'What happens if I do not meet them?', a: 'Rejection, not filtering. Gmail returns permanent 550 errors for non-compliant bulk mail rather than quietly moving it to spam, so the failure is loud and immediate.' },
  { q: 'Why do some items say "not checkable"?', a: 'Because they genuinely are not visible from DNS. Whether your platform negotiates TLS, whether your campaigns carry one-click unsubscribe headers, and what your spam-complaint rate is, are all facts no external tool can read. We say so rather than showing a green tick we have not earned.' },
  { q: 'How do I see my spam complaint rate?', a: 'Google Postmaster Tools, free, for Gmail; Microsoft SNDS for Outlook. If you send in volume and are not watching one of these, start there, the 0.3% threshold is measured by them, not by you.' },
];
---

<ToolPage
  title="Google and Yahoo Sender Requirements Checker | The MailDoc"
  description="Check your domain against the Google, Yahoo and Microsoft bulk sender requirements: SPF, DKIM, DMARC, alignment and reverse DNS, with an honest note on what cannot be checked from DNS."
  eyebrow="Sender Readiness"
  heading="Will the bulk sender rules reject you?"
  intro="Measured against what Google, Yahoo and Microsoft now require. Everything checkable from DNS is checked; everything that is not is labelled, not guessed."
  spec="the published Google, Yahoo and Microsoft sender guidelines"
  faqs={faqs}
  related={[
    { href: '/lab/spf-checker', label: 'SPF checker, one of the required records' },
    { href: '/lab/dkim-checker', label: 'DKIM checker, the other one' },
    { href: '/lab/reverse-dns', label: 'Reverse DNS, required of every sender' },
  ]}
>
  <Readiness slot="tool" client:load />

  <h2>Symptoms</h2>
  <p>
    Mail that used to deliver starts bouncing with a permanent error, usually all at once and usually
    to one provider. Bulk campaigns are refused while individual replies still arrive. Nothing in
    your setup changed. The requirements did.
  </p>

  <h2>Diagnosis</h2>
  <p>
    The large receivers agreed a shared baseline and now enforce it. It splits into two tiers.
  </p>
  <h3>Every sender</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>SPF or DKIM authentication</li>
    <li>Valid forward and reverse DNS on sending IPs</li>
    <li>TLS on transmission</li>
    <li>Correctly formatted messages</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Bulk senders, roughly 5,000 a day and up</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>SPF <em>and</em> DKIM</li>
    <li>A published DMARC policy, at minimum <code>p=none</code></li>
    <li>The From domain aligned with SPF or DKIM</li>
    <li>One-click unsubscribe in marketing mail</li>
    <li>Spam complaints below 0.3%</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Four of those cannot be determined from DNS by anyone. We mark them as unverifiable and point you
    at who can actually tell you, rather than counting them as passes to make the score look better.
  </p>

  <h2>Cure</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Publish and validate SPF and DKIM</strong> for every service that sends on your behalf.</li>
    <li><strong>Publish DMARC</strong>, read the reports, and fix whatever fails alignment.</li>
    <li><strong>Fix reverse DNS</strong> on any machine you run yourself.</li>
    <li><strong>Confirm the three invisible ones</strong> with your sending platform and Postmaster Tools.</li>
  </ol>
</ToolPage>
